---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/adamgreenwell/flume";

/**
 * The architecture rules, verbatim from CONTRIBUTING.md in the app repo.
 *
 * Reproduced rather than linked because they are the thing most likely to get
 * a first pull request rejected, and someone deciding whether to spend an
 * evening on this deserves to read them before they start rather than after.
 */
const rules = [
  {
    title: "No torrent binary data over IPC",
    body: "librqbit writes pieces to disk. The webview receives JSON only — progress, speeds, counts, file lists. This is the architecture's central claim, and everything else follows from it.",
  },
  {
    title: "The engine layer imports no Tauri types",
    body: "src-tauri/src/engine/ must compile and be testable under plain cargo test. If a change makes the engine need Tauri, the change is in the wrong layer.",
  },
  {
    title: "Command handlers are thin",
    body: "Logic worth testing belongs in the engine, where it can be tested without a webview or a mock runtime.",
  },
  {
    title: "Static export only",
    body: "No app/api routes, no middleware, no server-only functions. Everything reaches the backend through invoke, because there is no server to reach.",
  },
  {
    title: "Telemetry stays around 1 Hz, batched",
    body: "No per-piece events. The payload should scale with torrent count, never with piece count or file size.",
  },
  {
    title: "Shared types change together",
    body: "A serde struct in Rust and its TypeScript mirror in src/lib/ipc/types.ts change in the same commit. Not the next one.",
  },
  {
    title: "Minimal Tauri permissions",
    body: "Grant only what a feature needs. The shell plugin is not to be added without a discussion.",
  },
  {
    title: "Verdicts the data cannot support are not invented",
    body: "Where the engine reports nothing, the interface says so. A plausible number is worse than an honest blank, and the swarm verdicts exist precisely to hold that line.",
  },
];

const ways = [
  {
    label: "Run it on Windows or Linux",
    body: "Flume is developed on macOS. CI builds for every platform, but first-run behaviour on the others has never had a manual pass. This is the single most useful thing anyone can do right now, and it needs no Rust.",
    href: `${REPO}/issues/new/choose`,
    cta: "Report what you find",
  },
  {
    label: "Improve the documentation",
    body: "The documentation lives in docs/ in the app repo and is published to the wiki automatically on every push. Fix it at the source and the wiki follows.",
    href: `${REPO}/tree/main/docs`,
    cta: "Browse docs/",
  },
  {
    label: "Pick up an issue",
    body: "Issues carry the context needed to start. Anything blocked on an upstream decision says so, so you do not spend an evening on something that cannot land.",
    href: `${REPO}/issues`,
    cta: "Open issues",
  },
  {
    label: "Work on this website",
    body: "Separate repository, Astro, no Rust required. The design rules are written down and the quality gate is two commands.",
    href: "https://github.com/adamgreenwell/flume-site",
    cta: "Site repository",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Contribute"
  description="How to build Flume from source, the architecture rules a change is held to, and where to start."
>
  <section class="wrap head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Contribute</p>
    <h1 class="head__title">It builds in four commands.</h1>
    <p class="lead head__lead">
      Flume is Apache-2.0 and developed in the open. The engine is a dependency
      rather than a project, so almost everything worth doing is in the part
      people actually touch.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <h2 class="section__title">Building from source</h2>
        <p class="body">
          You need Rust 1.88 or later, Node 26, and your platform's Tauri system
          dependencies. The development setup page lists those per platform — on
          Debian and Ubuntu there are a handful of <span class="num">-dev</span> packages.
        </p>
        <pre
          class="code"><code>git clone {REPO}.git
cd flume
npm install
npm run tauri:dev</code></pre>
        <p class="body">
          Before opening a pull request, run the same gate CI runs. It is not a
          formality — a red gate is the most common reason a change sits.
        </p>
        <pre
          class="code"><code>npm run check
cd src-tauri
cargo fmt --check
cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo test</code></pre>
        <p class="note">
          Tests needing more than a checkout are <span class="num"
            >#[ignore]</span
          >d. A failure under <span class="num">--ignored</span> is not automatically
          a regression: the DHT tests want working internet, and the proxy test wants
          a SOCKS5 proxy on <span class="num">127.0.0.1:1080</span>. Each one's
          ignore reason names its own prerequisite.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="aside">
        <h2 class="eyebrow">Workflow</h2>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>Branch from <span class="num">main</span>.</li>
          <li>
            Small, frequent commits, in{" "}
            <a href="https://www.conventionalcommits.org" rel="noopener"
              >Conventional Commits</a
            > form.
          </li>
          <li>Open a pull request; CI runs the full gate.</li>
          <li>Merge when green.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="aside__note">
          History is treated as a deliverable rather than bookkeeping. A commit
          message that explains why a change was made is worth more than one
          that restates the diff.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Where to start</p>
      <h2 class="section__title">Four things that would genuinely help.</h2>
      <ul class="ways">
        {
          ways.map((way) => (
            <li class="way">
              <h3 class="way__title">{way.label}</h3>
              <p class="way__body">{way.body}</p>
              <a class="way__link" href={way.href} rel="noopener">
                {way.cta} →
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Non-negotiable</p>
    <h2 class="section__title">Eight rules a change is held to.</h2>
    <p class="section__lead">
      These are not style preferences. Violating one is a design defect, and a
      review will ask you to change it — so they are written down here rather
      than discovered in a pull request.
    </p>

    <ol class="rules">
      {
        rules.map((rule, i) => (
          <li class="rule">
            <span class="num rule__n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
            <div>
              <h3 class="rule__title">{rule.title}</h3>
              <p class="rule__body">{rule.body}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <p class="section__caveat">
      The interface has its own vocabulary, and it is equally firm: never
      introduce a colour that is not a token, status is never colour alone, and
      every number that sits in a column is set in tabular monospace. The{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/adamgreenwell/flume/wiki/Design-System"
        >design system</a
      > is the rulebook.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card">
        <h2 class="card__title">Reporting a security issue</h2>
        <p class="card__body">
          Please do not open a public issue. Use GitHub's private reporting on
          the Security tab. Note that some things people report are properties
          of BitTorrent rather than bugs — peers can see your IP, and DHT
          announces that you are in a swarm. The policy says which is which.
        </p>
        <a
          class="card__link"
          href={`${REPO}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}
          rel="noopener"
        >
          Security policy →
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h2 class="card__title">Code of Conduct</h2>
        <p class="card__body">
          The Contributor Covenant, and it is enforced. Everyone taking part —
          issues, pull requests, discussions — is held to it.
        </p>
        <a
          class="card__link"
          href={`${REPO}/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`}
          rel="noopener"
        >
          Read it →
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h2 class="card__title">What Flume will not do</h2>
        <p class="card__body">
          No cryptocurrency, no chat, no RSS automation, no paid tier, and no
          second CLI — <span class="num">rqbit</span> already exists. A proposal in
          those directions will be declined, so the roadmap says so in advance.
        </p>
        <a
          class="card__link"
          href="https://github.com/adamgreenwell/flume/wiki/Roadmap"
          >Roadmap →</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1rem + 5vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .head__title {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: var(--t-display);
  }

  .head__lead {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .section {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 1.5rem + 5vw, 5rem);
  }

  .section__title {
    font-size: var(--t-title);
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  .section__lead {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    color: var(--fg-1);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .section__caveat {
    margin-top: 2.25rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    max-width: 74ch;
  }

  .section__caveat a {
    color: var(--acc);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .band {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 1.5rem + 5vw, 5rem);
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 4rem);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }

  /* ---- build ---- */
  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 4vw, 3.5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .body {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--fg-1);
    max-width: 58ch;
  }

  .code {
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    background: var(--bg-1);
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    max-width: 58ch;
  }

  .aside {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }

  .steps {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }

  .steps::marker,
  .steps li::marker {
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  .steps a {
    color: var(--acc);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .aside__note {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    padding-top: 1.1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
  }

  /* ---- ways ---- */
  .ways {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .way {
    padding: 1.4rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: var(--bg-0);
  }

  .way__title {
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.014em;
  }

  .way__body {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }

  .way__link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    color: var(--acc);
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .way__link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* ---- rules ---- */
  .rules {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem 2.5rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: rule;
  }

  .rule {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.9rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .rule__n {
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--acc-dim);
    padding-top: 0.2em;
  }

  .rule__title {
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
  }

  .rule__body {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }

  /* ---- cards ---- */
  .cards {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .card {
    padding: 1.4rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }

  .card__title {
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.014em;
  }

  .card__body {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }

  .card__link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    color: var(--acc);
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .card__link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .split,
    .rules {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .ways {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
